Cell["\<\
So particle 9 in the diagram is a scalar with gauge representation {{0, 0}, \
{2}, 1} (color singlet, SU(2)L triplet, with hypercharge 1), 
particle 10 has the same quantum numbers as 9 (although we implicitly treat \
it as a separate particle, otherwise we\[CloseCurlyQuote]d have to make sure \
vertices involving 9 and 10 don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t vanish due to symmetry, in \
analogy to the Higgs case), particle 11 is a scalar with gauge representation \
{{0,1}, {0}, -2/3} (3-bar under color SU(3), SU(2)L singlet, with hypercharge \
-2/3), and particle 12 is a color triplet with hypercharge -4/3.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9361819710336037`*^9, 3.936182133208643*^9}, {
  3.936182170506996*^9, 3.936182237955751*^9}, {3.982160497451921*^9, 
  3.982160517791156*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4c362943-14a1-43b7-b11d-\
44ab6c5839c9"],

Cell["\<\
In other cases, we obtain multiple solutions; UVCompletion[...] is always a \
list of valid new-particle representations:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.936181630024136*^9, 3.936181639779896*^9}, 
   3.972126753204953*^9, {3.972126821005959*^9, 3.972126853998053*^9}, 
   3.972131415692724*^9, {3.972483598664386*^9, 3.972483607864357*^9}, 
   3.982160537749551*^9},ExpressionUUID->"1042ef08-a231-408f-8801-\
4baf8fef45ec"],

