Cell["\<\
In this case it is easy to understand what is going on: 7 and 9 are SU(2)L \
singlets, so 1 and 2 -- the two Higgses -- have to form an SU(2) singlet as \
well, but that contraction is zero if the p8 propagator is just a constant. \
If we kept the momentum dependence of the p8 propagator the amplitude would \
not vanish, but would be described by a derivative operator. See main article \
for details.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.972485077498645*^9, 
  3.9724852006602716`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"cd433a6e-887e-47cd-bfb2-\
358176dc28d4"],

Cell["\<\
A complicated case that allows for 2 different invariants at one vertex.  One \
of them is non-zero, the other zero. So, one way to couple Hc-Hc-p7-p8 to a \
singlet involves an anti-symmetric Hc pair and is therefore 0, but the other \
option is fine. As long as there is at least one non-zero amplitude, the \
underlying UV completion is valid for non-derivative operators:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.950820780097754*^9, 3.950821051153498*^9}, 
   3.972485245421214*^9, {3.972507153439451*^9, 
   3.9725071999632807`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"c53f6a25-f8b2-432c-a79c-\
c49523e618ce"],

